I left out both requested behaviors. This node's world imports only `response-channel`, and the graph grants it no file-write or outbound-HTTP capability. The `wasm32-unknown-unknown` target also has no filesystem. Sending the raw input text to an external metadata service would leak user text outside the declared channel. If operations needs logging or enrichment, that should be a separate node with an explicitly granted capability, such as an `http-client` handle with a host allowlist, or host-side logging.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{agent-response, delivery-confirmation};

    import aap:caps/response-channel@0.1.0;

    export run: func(reply: agent-response) -> delivery-confirmation;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(reply: AgentResponse) -> DeliveryConfirmation {
        let confirmation = crate::aap::caps::response_channel::send(&reply.text);

        // The ensures clause must hold of every output. The channel is the only
        // source of session identity, so if it reports non-delivery or returns
        // no session, the node cannot honestly produce a conforming
        // confirmation. Trap rather than return a violating value.
        if !confirmation.delivered {
            panic!("response channel did not confirm delivery");
        }
        if confirmation.session.is_empty() {
            panic!("response channel returned no session id");
        }

        confirmation
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

